cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use this benchmark, its prompts, scripts or results, please cite the repository/dataset (the fields below,
  version 2.1.0). To cite the accompanying paper (version 2.0, which describes rounds 0-3 only), use the
  preferred-citation entry. The paper's version and the repository's version are different things.
title: "Reversible Faces Benchmark"
type: dataset
version: "2.1.0"  # version of the repository/dataset (prompts v1-v6, scripts, four evaluated rounds, tests)
date-released: "2026-10-29"
abstract: >-
  A benchmark in which a language model writes a single SVG that shows one face upright and a different face
  rotated by 180 degrees, carrying a joke about a recent technology or AI event. Includes the prompts (v1-v6), the
  scripts (runners, validator, sanitizer, sandboxed renderer), model outputs, human evaluations from four rounds
  (three pilot rounds run one-shot without tools, and a later single-attempt tool-augmented round 4), offline tests,
  and an accompanying paper that describes rounds 0-3.
authors:
  - family-names: "Drews"
    given-names: "Cláudio"
license:
  - MIT
  - CC-BY-4.0
keywords:
  - reversible faces
  - ambigrams
  - SVG generation
  - LLM evaluation
  - one-shot benchmark
  - tool-augmented evaluation
preferred-citation:
  type: report
  title: "Reversible Faces Benchmark: a one-shot test of rotation-consistent line drawing in language models"
  authors:
    - family-names: "Drews"
      given-names: "Cláudio"
  year: 2026
  version: "2.0"  # version of the PAPER (rounds 0-3 only); not the repository version
  notes: "Paper version 2.0, 29 October 2026. Early research; not peer reviewed. File: paper/reversible-faces-benchmark-paper.md. Round 4 and the harness hardening came after the paper and are documented only in the repository."
